{
  "id": "halden-offsite-power-restoration",
  "title": "Halden: loss of offsite power",
  "description": "A summer night: four fictional nuclear units (pressurised-water reactors) at Halden export about 4.4 GW through one 420 kV switchyard, tied by three circuits each to Hasle and Tegneby. Two minutes in, a busbar fault opens all six circuits: the units lose offsite power, every turbine trips, and the grid frequency falls until under-frequency load shedding stops it. Restore offsite power by closing a Hasle circuit onto the dead switchyard, then the other circuits under synchro-check, and bring the grid back: raise hydro output and reconnect the shed load stage by stage. There are no weather-driven faults.",
  "objectives": [
    "Before the fault, note the export of the four unit bays, the switchyard voltage and the loading of the Hasle and Tegneby circuits and the NO1 corridors (connection-points.list, power-flow.snapshot).",
    "When the switchyard is isolated, confirm that its bays are de-energised (no voltage, no frequency) and that the Plants report loss of offsite power; follow the island frequency, the LFDD stages that operate and the reserves.",
    "Restore offsite power: close one Hasle circuit onto the dead switchyard (dead-bus closing), check that the bays are energised and the Plants draw station service from the grid, then close the remaining five circuits; synchro-check limits the voltage-angle difference across each loop you close.",
    "Restore the grid: raise hydro output until the frequency is back within the \u00b1100 mHz standard range, then reconnect the load that automatic under-frequency shedding cut, one stage at a time, without another under-frequency excursion, and re-arm each stage."
  ],
